WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.766
Mission planning begins with a concise purpose.

00:00:03.766 --> 00:00:12.850
Define what the flight must accomplish, the data required, who is responsible, and what would make the mission complete.

00:00:12.850 --> 00:00:17.946
A clear purpose prevents unnecessary flight time and excessive collection.

00:00:17.946 --> 00:00:21.713
Review the location using current, authoritative information.

00:00:21.713 --> 00:00:30.797
Consider applicable airspace, nearby airports or heliports, temporary restrictions, terrain, structures, wires, trees, vehicles, people, animals, and property boundaries.

00:00:30.797 --> 00:00:35.449
Do not rely only on an old saved map.

00:00:35.449 --> 00:00:38.330
Weather affects control and endurance.

00:00:38.330 --> 00:00:45.198
Review wind, gusts, precipitation, visibility, temperature, and changing conditions for the entire operating period.

00:00:45.198 --> 00:00:51.623
Compare conditions with aircraft limitations and operator experience, then include a conservative margin.

00:00:51.623 --> 00:00:57.162
Plan battery use for launch, mission work, return, contingencies, and landing.

00:00:57.162 --> 00:01:04.030
Identify a clear launch and recovery area with room to manage people and equipment.

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:09.569
Avoid planning that depends on using nearly all available battery capacity.

00:01:09.569 --> 00:01:12.450
Choose emergency options before takeoff.

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:21.533
Identify safe landing areas, loss-of-link behavior, low-battery actions, crew calls, and conditions that trigger an immediate return or landing.

00:01:21.533 --> 00:01:26.629
Confirm that automated return settings match the local obstacle environment.

00:01:26.629 --> 00:01:31.725
Document the plan at a level appropriate to the mission.

00:01:31.725 --> 00:01:39.480
Include location, purpose, crew, aircraft, permissions, weather, airspace review, hazards, controls, battery plan, and emergency actions.

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:46.791
Planning does not eliminate uncertainty; it gives the team a disciplined way to manage it.
